WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Film Courage: Do villains have defining moments?

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter, Author, and&nbsp;
Instructor: I think that they certainly can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.440
Sometimes we get villains who&nbsp;
are very cardboard cut out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:24.560
just gleefully wreaking havoc for the heck of&nbsp;
it. I think of a classic film Spider-Man 2 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:32.640
there Doc Ock has a defining moment. He isn’t&nbsp;
evil for the sake of evil, he is a man who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:41.840
wounded through the death of his wife, that's&nbsp;
the moment that defines him. In Spider-Man 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.640
written by the same writer who wrote&nbsp;
Ordinary People [Alvin Sargent]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.360
we get a villain who actually has an&nbsp;
arc and who transforms and in the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:06.320
is able to take a step back from the&nbsp;
evil and to actually sacrifice himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.080
to join with Spider-Man and to save the&nbsp;
city. So yes, and I think that Spider-Man 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.600
plays a really important role in the&nbsp;
history of superhero action films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:33.200
We look back and we may not recognize that because&nbsp;
we're used to superhero action films that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:44.800
rich character development but Spider-Man 2&nbsp;
really took that up a huge step by in part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:52.080
looking deeply in to the character&nbsp;
of their villain and by providing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.600
the depth and complexity and&nbsp;
dimension that defining moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:10.080
afforded that character and when we do see these&nbsp;
defining moments for these villains is it to to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:17.040
have the audience turn to to have some empathy&nbsp;
toward that antagonist or is it just to further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:25.360
understand the arc of the story I think the effect&nbsp;
is that we actually do have some empathy for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:32.720
villain and and it's not to say that we like the&nbsp;
villain or we agree with the evil the villain does

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:43.120
but it's to say that we recognize the villain's&nbsp;
humanity and we say oh you are at least a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.800
little bit like me we are the same species and&nbsp;
there are a lot of different ways to approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:56.960
villains we can approach villains as other just&nbsp;
completely different from me and outside of me but

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:07.920
you think about Pinocchio so if we go way back&nbsp;
to a very disturbing children's animated film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:16.720
a scene where Pinocchio is at the&nbsp;
pool hall and he and the other boys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:23.520
are drinking and smoking cigars and playing&nbsp;
pool and being transformed into donkeys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.840
that's a vision of evil that&nbsp;
says oh there is no difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:45.760
between us and them we are them and that is a&nbsp;
cautionary tale but it also does lead to a kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:54.160
of empathy where we can recognize oh that villain&nbsp;
is really broken and what they're doing may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:05.760
horrible and actually we may call it evil but they&nbsp;
are also one of us and for my storytelling money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:13.840
that's that's closer to what I resonate&nbsp;
with as like yeah I think that's how it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:20.320
how are the decisions of the villain different&nbsp;
from the hero in many ways the hero and villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:27.840
decisions are the same they're driven by the&nbsp;
way they see the world and what they want when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:36.560
am writing an antagonist and I will often think&nbsp;
in terms of antagonist as opposed to villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:46.960
because villain is more condemning than I think&nbsp;
i'm i'm ready to be with most of my characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.840
Joseph Mengele certainly was a villain and

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:04.880
did pure evil and he was a human which is what&nbsp;
made him so interesting to me as a character

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.760
what allowed us to write&nbsp;
Joseph Mengele in a way that

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:20.400
I think was so disturbing and&nbsp;
ended up getting the actor

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.320
best actor nomination for the European film award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:39.440
was that we approached him as a character who&nbsp;
thought he was the hero and I think that everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:46.400
sees themselves as the hero of their story they&nbsp;
do what they do for the reasons they do them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.640
and even when they do something that they&nbsp;
would recognize is like okay that was bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.840
but you have to understand I had a really&nbsp;
good justification why I had to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.640
it's part of the genius of Michael Corleone's&nbsp;
character we understand with every choice he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.320
makes why he makes it and we cringe because&nbsp;
sometimes he's killing family members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.240
but we always recognize the reasons&nbsp;
that he does it and it creates this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.440
cognitive dissonance for us&nbsp;
because we're saying oh no that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.240
like don't you shouldn't do that and at the same&nbsp;
time I totally understand why you just did that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:40.960
I think that gives us really fascinating&nbsp;
antagonists and villains if we do we do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:48.240
same character development for them that we do&nbsp;
for any other character I don't think that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:55.280
we have to have really fuzzy moral judgment&nbsp;
I think we can still recognize evil as evil

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.520
and recognize the humanity of the people&nbsp;
who are doing the evil at the same time it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:12.080
a more nuanced approach I think it's more&nbsp;
interesting and I think again I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:20.160
it rings true to me for how people actually are I&nbsp;
haven't like I don't think I've ever met a villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:29.840
at Von’s I’ve met people doing dumb things&nbsp;
and being irresponsible and uncaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.280
but they think they're right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:47.760
so I try to approach all my characters&nbsp;
with love even the ones that are wicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:55.120
how do writers get audiences to care about&nbsp;
the villains I think that on the one hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:03.280
you could get away with the audience not caring&nbsp;
about the villains even gleefully rooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.760
for the destruction of the villain

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:21.840
but the example of 2 shows us that there is&nbsp;
emotional gold to be mined in providing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:33.680
glimpse into the humanity of a villain so the in&nbsp;
the same way that we would reveal a character say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:39.840
a heroic character to the audience so that they&nbsp;
can understand where that character's coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:49.120
we can use those same tools to reveal&nbsp;
who this villain is and why this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:56.240
villain is how what journey this character&nbsp;
has been on to arrive at their villainy

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:12.240
the I think the emotional experience then&nbsp;
becomes really complex because now I am

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:18.640
I recognize that character has to be stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.080
i'm thinking of the camp commandant in&nbsp;
Schindler's List where we really do get

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:34.960
some insights into this character into his&nbsp;
vulnerability into there's there's a moment where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:46.720
he toys with showing mercy to people and then&nbsp;
he pulls back from that it's like that's not me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.840
i'm not i'm not going to&nbsp;
be that guy who is merciful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.600
but the fact that he had that moment&nbsp;
where he could have been merciful

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.640
colors the way we feel about him when&nbsp;
he's executed at the end of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:12.720
it makes it more complicated in a way that I think&nbsp;
enriches the experience never confuses us about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:21.200
the evil that he's perpetrated the film never&nbsp;
has any confusion about that the evil is evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.800
but it does show us the complexity of&nbsp;
the person perpetrating the evil which

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:38.960
does I think make for a more&nbsp;
interesting storytelling experience.

